# Enterprise Architecture Implementation Plan

This document outlines the strategy to transform StitchMaster AI from a single-company internal tool into a massively scalable, autonomous, multi-tenant B2B SaaS platform.

> [!WARNING]  
> Implementing all 5 features requires a fundamental rewrite of the database schema, API security, and AI orchestration layer. We must execute this in strictly defined phases to prevent data loss or system collapse.

## User Review Required

Please review the proposed phases below. **Phase 1 (Multi-Tenant Architecture)** is a breaking change that will alter every single database query in the application. It is recommended to complete Phase 1 first so that all subsequent features are natively multi-tenant.

## Open Questions

1. **Embedding Provider:** Groq currently focuses on LLMs and does not provide an embedding model API. For the Vector Database (RAG), should we integrate OpenAI (e.g., `text-embedding-3-small`), Cohere, or use a local open-source embedding model (e.g., HuggingFace transformers) to generate the vector arrays?
2. **Email Provider:** For the Predictive Procurement (Auto-drafting POs) and Workflow Agents (Customer Notification), do you have a preferred email provider (SendGrid, Postmark, AWS SES, or Nodemailer with SMTP)?
3. **Existing Data Migration:** When we switch to Multi-Tenant (Phase 1), should all existing users, orders, and inventory be migrated into a single default "Legacy Tenant", or should the database be wiped for a clean SaaS slate?

